WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.831
Where P is the principal amount borrowed,
A is the periodic amortization payment, r

00:00:05.831 --> 00:00:08.230
is the periodic interest rate divided by 100…

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:10.990
Philip, I think it’s a little early for
this...

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.289
Oh yea, this is maybe more bedtime reading.

00:00:14.289 --> 00:00:15.289
Not early in the day!

00:00:15.289 --> 00:00:16.860
Early in her life.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.580
I just want her to have a good headstart.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.670
They say kids who learn about money early
on develop better financial habits as adults.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.910
But she’s only three months old!

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.430
You mean one fiscal quarter old.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.220
I’m putting her down for a nap.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:46.820
A recent study found that 44% of Americans
consider personal finance to be the most difficult

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:52.859
topic to discuss with others, moreso than
politics, religion or death.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:58.740
Maybe that’s why most parents are more comfortable
discussing “birds and bees” than “terms and fees.”

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.340
See what i did there?

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.930
And when parents do discuss finances with
their children, they tend to limit it to simple

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.930
ideas like saving, spending, and earning.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:13.100
Researchers at North Carolina University found
that children sensed that certain topics were

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.119
“off-limits,” including family finances,
parental income, investments and debt.

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:21.810
If investments and debt were discussed at
all, they were far more likely to be discussed

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.789
with boys and not girls.

00:01:23.789 --> 00:01:27.750
And most children had no idea why their parents
were secretive about these things.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:32.330
The truth is that money management is one
of the most useful skills for getting by in

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:38.590
our modern world, and yet one that many Americans
are never formally taught--not in school or

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:39.659
from their parents.

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:43.300
So who does teach today’s children about
money?

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.300
Advertisers.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.320
Who, believe it or not, might not have your
family’s best interests at heart.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.579
Unfortunately, there are a lot of predatory
organizations out there that make big profits

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.340
from other people’s poor financial decisions.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:02.040
Don’t get us wrong, making mistakes is an
invaluable part of the learning process.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:05.499
But when you’re young, mistakes don’t
hurt that much.

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:10.280
If your lemonade stand doesn’t sell enough
units to cover overhead, well, you’re out

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.750
a couple bucks, but you’ve learned an important
lesson on managing cash flow.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.970
That same lesson in your twenties will cost
you a heck of a lot more than a couple bucks.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.980
In fact, it could set you on a slippery slope
to lifelong debt.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.620
A study by by Edutopia found that kids who
were taught financial literacy tended to have

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.410
less debt and a higher net worth as adults.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:35.660
Now we’re not suggesting you run off and
discuss correlation coefficients with your first grader!

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:41.180
As your child’s brain develops, certain
topics will be more age appropriate than others.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:45.950
When a child is between 4 and 6 they’re
ready to be introduced to the basic concepts

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:51.319
of money: Money buys things, it can be earned,
and some people have more than others.

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:56.629
Games that use coins or paper currency can
be a great way to make money seem less abstract

00:02:56.629 --> 00:02:58.720
and more concrete.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.560
At around 6 to 9 years old, children can begin
interacting with money in the real world.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.319
You might decide to give them a regular allowance,
or “pay” them for doing extra chores

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:09.569
or getting good grades.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:13.350
This is also a good time to encourage giving.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:18.120
Help them pick a charity that they like, and
take them to make a donation in person, to

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.230
see the human impact of their gift.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:26.870
Between 10-15, kids become able to absorb
more advanced financial concepts, like borrowing

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:28.120
and investing.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:33.060
If your child has their heart set on a big
purchase, you might consider loaning them the money.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.400
You could even consider charging them interest
(and putting it in their college funds).

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.599
Better they learn the concept from you than
a credit card company.

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:43.470
Alternatively, if they’ve managed to save
some money, help them research investment

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:47.180
options like a CD, savings bond, or index
fund.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.200
Nothing can get them excited about finances
like watching their money grow!

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.890
As they become a teenager, you should begin
to reveal greater details of your own financial

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.180
habits and situation.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.240
I know, it seems… weird.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.900
But frank discussions about your salary, mortgage,
taxes--even debt--could give them a huge leg

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.160
up as adults.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.300
If there's something in your finances you're not so proud of well...

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:14.240
There’s nothing more natural than kids learning
from their parents’ mistakes.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:19.440
It’s also the best way to familiarize them
with the logistics of money management: ATM’s,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.960
digital banking, accounting apps.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:26.080
Show them how you actually keep track of your
balances and pay your bills.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.320
Most people have to figure that stuff out
all on their own, but it doesn’t have to be that way.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.380
Remember, you don’t have to be a perfect
teacher.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:36.850
Many parents don’t bring up the topic of
money because they don’t want to say the

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.820
wrong thing, but any kind of introduction
is better than none at all.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.669
One way or another, your kids will learn these
lessons.

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:48.220
Wouldn’t you rather it come from someone
who genuinely wants what’s best for them?

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.820
And that’s our two cents!

